\chapter{Servers as programs}
\label{ch:servers}

Up to now the two servers of Ulix, telnet and SSH, are part of the kernel: the network thread accepts the connections, speaks the
protocol and feeds the keys into the terminal. The chapter on daemons says why that is only a shortcut: a server that runs in the
kernel can crash the system, its mistakes cost kernel memory, and the cryptography of the SSH server has to fit on the 16~KByte stack
of a kernel thread. A server should be a \emph{program} that the administrator starts with \texttt{service}, that the kernel can
end like any other process, and that has a normal stack.

Such a program needs three things from the kernel that it did not have:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Server sockets}: a way to say ``I accept connections to port 23'' and to wait for the next one. That is the first
  section of this chapter.
\item \emph{A way to attach a session to a terminal}: what the kernel's telnet code did with \texttt{telnet\_input} and
  \texttt{telnet\_putch}, as system calls. That is the second section (a minimal pseudo terminal).
\item \emph{A convention for starting}: the files in \texttt{/etc/rc.d}, which the chapter on daemons describes.
\end{enumerate}

The TCP code of the kernel does not change: it has always been able to accept connections (\texttt{tcp\_listen} and
\texttt{tcp\_accept} in \texttt{tcp.c}), the kernel servers used it. This chapter only puts system calls on top.

\section{Server sockets: \texttt{tcp\_listen} and \texttt{tcp\_accept}}
\label{src:servers:listen}

A program that connects to a server already has the system calls \texttt{tcp\_connect}, \texttt{tcp\_send}, \texttt{tcp\_recv} and
\texttt{tcp\_close}. They work with a small number, the \emph{socket}: a slot in a table of the kernel that points to a connection. The
server side needs two more calls, and the rest stays the same: a connection that was accepted is a socket like any other.

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    tcp_listen (port)                accept connections to this port: 0 = ok, -1 = no room, -2 = only root may use ports below 1024 (630)
    tcp_accept (port, timeout_ms)    wait for the next client: a socket (then tcp_send, tcp_recv, tcp_close),
                                     -1 = no room for another socket, -2 = time over (631)
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

\paragraph{Why the port and not a socket number.} The Unix \texttt{listen} works on a socket that \texttt{socket} and \texttt{bind} have
made before. The TCP code of Ulix knows no such object: a listener is only a port number in a table. A call that names the port is
all it takes, and a student can see at once what it does. (\texttt{socket}, \texttt{bind} and friends are a matter for the Linux
interface of the kernel, which has to offer the system calls of musl anyway; they are not part of this step.) The price: two programs cannot listen on the same
port, and a second \texttt{tcp\_listen} for a port that is already open succeeds without doing anything. That is what lets a server
that was ended and started again take over its port. (A port stays open after its server has ended; connections that arrive in the
meantime wait for the next server.)

\paragraph{Only root opens low ports.} As on every Unix, ports below 1024 are reserved for the administrator: whoever listens on port
23 or 22 decides what a user's password is typed into. The system call looks at the effective user ID (the same test as \texttt{net\_set}).

\paragraph{\texttt{tcp\_accept} sleeps.} A server spends nearly all its time waiting for the next client, and a program that waits in
a loop (\texttt{yield}, look, \texttt{yield}, \ldots) uses a CPU for nothing. The process therefore \emph{sleeps} in a blocked queue,
as a process that waits for the disk or for a pipe. Who wakes it? Everything that can end the wait comes in through the network card,
and the network thread looks at the card whenever the card has something and at least twenty times a second. After each round it
wakes everybody who waits for the network (\texttt{net\_wake\_all}), and each of them looks whether its own condition is true now. That is
the same idea as the one queue of the pipes: a wrong guess costs one look. A wake-up that falls between the look and the sleep is not lost
for long, because the next round of the network thread is at most 50 milliseconds away.

The same waiting is used by \texttt{tcp\_recv}, which used to poll in a loop. That was the shortcut of the first TLS program; it is not
good enough for a server that has a connection open for hours.

\paragraph{A signal does not interrupt the sleep.} A process that gets \texttt{SIGKILL} while it sleeps must die at once, so it leaves
the queue first (\texttt{net\_before\_kill}, called from \texttt{u\_kill} like \texttt{pipes\_before\_kill}). A signal that has a handler
(\texttt{SIGTERM} of a daemon) is only seen when the system call returns. So a server waits with a time limit of a second and looks at its
flag after every wait. The system call has no \texttt{EINTR}; to add one, the sleeping loops would need to know whether a signal is pending, and
that is a change in the book's signal code that this step does not need.

<<net_syscalls.c: waiting>>=
#define TSTATE_WAITNET 14                    // a process sleeps in net_queue (12 is the serial disk, 13 a pipe)

static blocked_queue net_queue;              // everybody who waits for the network: accept, recv

// a process may only sleep if it is a real process: the idle thread and the boot code poll (see the book)
static int net_can_sleep (void) { return current_task > 1 && scheduler_is_active; }

void net_wake_all (void) {                   // the network thread has looked at the card: the waiters look at their condition again
  while (net_queue.next != 0) deblock (net_queue.next, &net_queue);
}

// a process that is killed while it sleeps here must leave the queue first (the book's u_kill knows only its own queues)
void net_before_kill (int pid) {
  if (pid > 1 && pid < MAX_THREADS && thread_table[pid].used && thread_table[pid].state == TSTATE_WAITNET) deblock (pid, &net_queue);
}

// sleep until the network thread has made a round (the caller then looks again); without a scheduler: let the others run
static void net_wait (void) {
  if (!net_can_sleep ()) { arch_resign ();  return; }
  block (&net_queue, TSTATE_WAITNET);
  arch_resign ();                            // here the process sleeps
}
@

First the declarations that the file needs in addition (the TCP functions are the ones of [[tcp.c]]; [[tcp_connect]] and the others are declared in the chapter on TLS):

<<net_syscalls.c: servers>>=
extern int       tcp_listen (unsigned short port);
extern tcp_conn *tcp_accept (unsigned short port);
@

The two system calls. \texttt{tcp\_accept} first needs a free socket slot (\texttt{free\_socket} also closes the sockets of processes that
have ended); it then asks the TCP code for a new connection to the port and sleeps while there is none. A timeout below zero waits
for ever.

<<net_syscalls.c: servers>>=
void syscall_tcp_listen (context_t *r) {
  int port = (int)r->ebx;
  if (port < 1 || port > 65535) eax_return (-1);
  if (port < 1024 && !is_root ()) eax_return (-2);
  eax_return (tcp_listen (port));
}

void syscall_tcp_accept (context_t *r) {
  int port = (int)r->ebx, timeout_ms = (int)r->ecx;
  int h = free_socket ();
  if (h < 0) eax_return (-1);
  unsigned long long start = net_now_ms ();
  for (;;) {
    tcp_conn *c = tcp_accept (port);
    if (c) { socks[h].c = c;  socks[h].id = tcp_id (c);  socks[h].owner = current_task;  eax_return (h); }
    if (timeout_ms >= 0 && net_now_ms () - start >= (unsigned)timeout_ms) eax_return (-2);
    net_poll_once ();
    net_wait ();
  }
}

@
\paragraph{The user side.} The wrappers join the header that the network programs use. They take the arguments in registers, like
\texttt{getrandom}, so no structure is needed.

<<userland/arm64-extra.h: servers>>=
// server sockets (net_syscalls.c, chapter "Servers as programs")
#define __NR_tcp_listen   630
#define __NR_tcp_accept   631
static inline int tcp_listen (int port)                  { return syscall2 (__NR_tcp_listen, port); }                       // 0 = ok, -1 = no room, -2 = only root may use ports below 1024
static inline int tcp_accept (int port, int timeout_ms)  { return syscall3 (__NR_tcp_accept, port, timeout_ms); }           // a socket, -1 = no room, -2 = time over; timeout -1: wait for ever
@

\section{A test server: \texttt{echod}}
\label{src:userland:echod.c}

The smallest server that is not a toy answers every byte with the same byte (RFC 862, \emph{echo}). \texttt{echod PORT} listens, accepts one client
at a time, sends back what it receives until the client hangs up, and goes back to \texttt{accept}. It is a service as well, with
\texttt{/etc/rc.d/echod}: switched off unless \texttt{rc.conf} says \texttt{echod\_enable="YES"}.

Two things show how a server is written. The main loop waits for a client with a time limit of one second, not for ever, and looks at
a flag after every wait, because \texttt{SIGTERM} (what \texttt{service stop} sends) only makes the handler run when the system call
returns. And a client that has gone must not leave the server stuck: \texttt{tcp\_recv} says $-1$ and the server closes its end.

<<userland/echod.c>>=
#include "../ulixlib.h"
#include "../arm64-extra.h"

static volatile int stop = 0;
static void on_term (int signo) { stop = 1; }              // SIGTERM: end after the current wait

static void echo (int sock) {                              // everything that arrives goes back, until the client hangs up
  unsigned char buf[512];
  struct tcp_msg m;
  while (!stop) {
    m.handle = sock;  m.buf = buf;  m.len = sizeof buf;  m.timeout_ms = 1000;
    int n = tcp_recv (&m);
    if (n < 0) return;                                     // closed
    if (n == 0) continue;                                  // nothing for a second: look at the flag
    m.len = n;  m.timeout_ms = 10000;
    if (tcp_send (&m) < 0) return;
  }
}

int main (int argc, char **argv) {
  int port = argc > 1 ? atoi (argv[1]) : 7007;
  signal (SIGTERM, on_term);
  if (tcp_listen (port) != 0) { printf ("echod: cannot listen on port %d\n", port);  return 1; }
  while (!stop) {
    int sock = tcp_accept (port, 1000);                    // sleeps until a client comes (at most a second)
    if (sock == -1) { waitkey (100);  continue; }          // no room for a socket: try again soon
    if (sock < 0) continue;                                // time over
    echo (sock);
    tcp_close (sock);
  }
  return 0;
}
@

<<etc/rc.d/echod>>=
# echod: the echo server (a test and an example of a server written as a program); listens on port 7007
command="/bin/echod 7007"
rcvar="echod_enable"
default="NO"
pidfile="/var/run/echod.pid"
@

\section{The test}

\texttt{make listen-test} (\texttt{tools/listen\_test.py}) boots the shell kernel with a network card, on a copy of the disk whose
\texttt{rc.conf} switches \texttt{echod} on. The boot starts the service before the login appears, and qemu forwards a port of the host
to port 7007 of the kernel. The test then behaves like clients: one that sends text and gets it back, a second one that comes while the
first one is still connected (it has to wait: the server serves one client at a time, and the queue of connections is the one of TCP), one
that connects and leaves without a word, and one that sends 20000 bytes (more than the buffers of TCP hold). A boot with the default
(\texttt{echod} off) must not answer on the port.
